  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 PM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nGOES vis satellite imagery shows mostly clear skies with a few high \nclouds drifting across the U.P. this afternoon. A weak wave in the \nupper flow is currently drifting across the U.P., but the upper \nridge will quickly strengthen tonight. And, surface high pressure \nremains centered over the Ohio Valley. This pattern puts the U.P. in \na prolonged period of warm southwesterly flow through Saturday. \nBreezy southwest winds gusting to 15 to 20 mph each day equates to \nabove normal daytime highs in the 50s. Meanwhile, the overnights \nwill also be balmy with lows in the 30s and 40s. \n\nBy Thursday, a closed upper low originating in the Rockies will make \nits way into the Upper Midwest, bringing a few rain showers to the \narea (mostly over Lake Superior/northern portions of U.P.). Latest \nmodel runs have continued to trend toward a dry forecast across most \nof the U.P. Nonetheless, Thursday will be a windy day, especially \nfor the northern Keweenaw where southwest gusts could top off above \n30 mph. \n\nFurther out, focus then turns to a stronger cutoff low over the \nIntermountain West that eventually phases with a low drifting \ndown from northern Saskatchewan. Once again, the U.P. will get \ncaught up in the warm sector of this on Saturday as high \ntemperatures climb to near the 60 degree mark. A cold front, \nhowever, will abruptly come through Saturday night, though, \nbringing high temperatures back down into the 30s/40s on Sunday \nand even as cold as upper 20s/low 30s for Monday. Northwest flow\nin the wake of the system will also help to transition rain to \nsnow through Sunday. But, there is still much uncertainty in \ntiming, tracks, and amounts this far out in the forecast. So, \ncontinue to monitor for any snow potential going into the \nThanksgiving week.",
